<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Awami League</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1949">1949</a>–</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Bangladesh Awami League</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Awami League is a political party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangladesh">Bangladesh</a>, founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhaka">Dhaka</a> in 1949 as a rival to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/all-india-muslim-league">Muslim League</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-pakistan">East Pakistan</a>. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sheikh-mujibur-rahman">Sheikh Mujibur Rahman</a> it campaigned for Bengali autonomy, won the 1970 general election and led the independence struggle of 1971. Led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sheikh-hasina">Sheikh Hasina</a>, it governed from 1996 to 2001 and from 2009 until she was forced from office in 2024, after which its activities were banned.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/awami-league.jpg" alt="Awami League" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nirvik12 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E0%A6%AC%E0%A6%BE%E0%A6%82%E0%A6%B2%E0%A6%BE%E0%A6%A6%E0%A7%87%E0%A6%B6_%E0%A6%86%E0%A6%93%E0%A6%AF%E0%A6%BC%E0%A6%BE%E0%A6%AE%E0%A7%80_%E0%A6%B2%E0%A7%80%E0%A6%97%E0%A7%87%E0%A6%B0_%E0%A6%AC%E0%A7%8D%E0%A6%AF%E0%A6%BE%E0%A6%9C.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The party was founded on 23 June 1949 as the East Pakistan Awami Muslim League, a breakaway faction of the Muslim League formed within two years of the creation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>. Its founders were Abdul Hamid Khan Bhashani, Yar Mohammad Khan, Shamsul Huq and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sheikh-mujibur-rahman">Sheikh Mujibur Rahman</a>. The word Muslim was dropped in 1953 and it became a secular party, and after 1955 it was the East Pakistan Awami League, the provincial party of the All-Pakistan Awami League founded by Huseyn Suhrawardy as an alternative to the dominance of the Muslim League.</p><p>Awami comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urdu">Urdu</a> word awam, meaning people, so the name can be translated as Bangladesh People's League. The party quickly gained wide support in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-pakistan">East Pakistan</a> and came to lead Bengali nationalism against the military and political establishment of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-pakistan">West Pakistan</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The six-point demands proposed by Mujib in 1966 asked for greater autonomy for the provinces and were widely accepted in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-pakistan">East Pakistan</a>. In the elections of 1970 the Awami League won 167 of 169 East Pakistan seats in the National Assembly and none of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-pakistan">West Pakistan</a>'s 138, which gave it a majority in the 313-seat house and the means to form a government without partners. This was unacceptable to West Pakistan's political leaders and led directly to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangladesh-liberation-war">Bangladesh Liberation War</a>. Mujib was arrested on 25 March 1971, and the war continued for nine months, until victory on 16 December 1971.</p><p>The party formed the first government of independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangladesh">Bangladesh</a>, which faced mine clearing, food shortages and a famine in 1974 in which 70,000 people died. In January 1975 the Awami League and other left-wing parties were merged into the Bangladesh Krishak Sramik Awami League. After the coup of 15 August 1975 the party was pushed to the political sidelines, and many senior leaders and activists were executed or jailed. In 1981 Mujib's daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sheikh-hasina">Sheikh Hasina</a> became president of the party, and she united its rival factions. The party opposed the regime of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hussain-muhammad-ershad">Hussain Muhammad Ershad</a> and emerged as a principal force after the restoration of democracy in 1990.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Awami League” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Grievances over language, economics and political power grew, and when the <b>Awami League</b> under Sheikh Mujibur Rahman won the 1970 election but was denied office, the Pakistani army cracked down in March 1971.</blockquote><a href="/state/bangladesh">Bangladesh</a></li></ul></section>
